{"authored" [{"question" "q-3742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$4-\\frac{(-2)}{q}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/state.json" "file_sha256" "25a8d74ef2f25f2e11cad5ff5b3c8cd2302c2a767650593a9cd27f03d529066f" "basis" 319 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-3742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the fact that $4=\\frac{4}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}-\\frac{(-2)}{q}=.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $q$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{1}-\\frac{(-2)}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{q}{q}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1}-\\frac{(-2)}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{4q}{q}-\\frac{(-2)}{q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4q}{q}-\\frac{(-2)}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{4q-(-2)}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{4q+2}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(2q+1)}{q}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/state.json" "file_sha256" "25a8d74ef2f25f2e11cad5ff5b3c8cd2302c2a767650593a9cd27f03d529066f" "basis" 319 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-3742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdd97fb72cc78cc05c3db63fb0e5f5cc9787dc127dcf8f8322efb3a286f7b38a" "basis" 319 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/math