capture/13930176/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-70295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\frac{1}{z-1}+\\frac{2w}{2w-3}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/state.json" "file_sha256" "25a8d74ef2f25f2e11cad5ff5b3c8cd2302c2a767650593a9cd27f03d529066f" "basis" 319 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-70295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $(z-1)(2w-3),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit. \n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $2w-3,$ and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $z-1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{z-1}+\\frac{2w}{2w-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2w-3}{2w-3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{z-1}+\\frac{z-1}{z-1}\\cdot \\frac{2w}{2w-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2w-3}{(z-1)(2w-3)}+\\frac{(z-1)2w}{(z-1)(2w-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(2w-3)+(z-1)2w}{(z-1)(2w-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2w-3+2wz-2w}{(z-1)(2w-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2wz-3}{(z-1)(2w-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/state.json" "file_sha256" "25a8d74ef2f25f2e11cad5ff5b3c8cd2302c2a767650593a9cd27f03d529066f" "basis" 319 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-70295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bdd97fb72cc78cc05c3db63fb0e5f5cc9787dc127dcf8f8322efb3a286f7b38a" "basis" 319 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacad