ture/13930176/state.json" "file_sha256" "25a8d74ef2f25f2e11cad5ff5b3c8cd2302c2a767650593a9cd27f03d529066f" "basis" 319 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Write $\\frac{3w}{w+4}-6$ as a single fraction." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/state.json" "file_sha256" "25a8d74ef2f25f2e11cad5ff5b3c8cd2302c2a767650593a9cd27f03d529066f" "basis" 319 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the fact that $6=\\frac{6}{1}$, we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6}{1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $w+4$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the second fraction by $w+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6}{1} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{w+4}{w+4}\\cdot \\frac{6}{1} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6(w+4)}{w+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6(w+4)}{w+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w-6(w+4)}{w+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w-6w-24}{w+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{-3w-24}{w+4} & = \\\\ -\\frac{3(w+8)}{w+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/state.json" "file_sha256" "25a8d74ef2f25f2e11cad5ff5b3c8cd2302c2a767650593a9cd27f03d529066f" "basis" 319 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930176/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "v